<Base title="Kinds of work in Australia — Career Central">
  <div class="prose">
  <h1>Kinds of work in Australia</h1>
  <p>
    Two people with the same job title can end up with completely different
    pay, leave, and security — because "job" actually covers a few legally
    different arrangements, each with its own trade-offs, and knowing which
    one you've been offered changes what you should check before you
    accept.
  </p>
  <p>
    Work out which of the two broad categories below you're actually being
    offered, then check the specific type against what you were told.
  </p>

  <h2>Employee arrangements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Casual</strong> — paid per hour worked, usually with a casual
      loading on top of the base rate to compensate for no guaranteed hours
      and no paid leave. Flexible around a timetable, but income varies week
      to week.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Part-time / full-time (ongoing)</strong> — a set minimum number
      of hours, with paid annual and personal (sick) leave, and a notice
      period if either side ends it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fixed-term contract</strong> — ongoing-style entitlements
      (pro-rata leave, notice), but for a set period or until a project ends,
      not indefinitely.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Graduate programs</strong> — structured one-to-two year programs
      at larger employers, usually with rotations across teams; applications
      typically open around a year before you'd start, well before you finish
      your degree.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Internships &amp; clerkships</strong> — can be paid or unpaid,
      but unpaid is only lawful where it's a genuine, structured vocational
      placement tied to your course (for course credit). If you're doing real
      productive work with no course link, it should be paid — that's a Fair
      Work Ombudsman matter, not a judgement call for the employer to make
      unilaterally.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Not-quite-employee arrangements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Independent contracting (ABN-based)</strong> — you invoice for
      your own business; no employee entitlements (leave, minimum wage
      protections in the usual sense), but more control over how and when you
      work. See <a href="/guides/getting-set-up/">the ABN guide</a> before
      agreeing to this arrangement for what should be an ordinary job.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Gig-platform work</strong> (food delivery, rideshare, and
      similar) — usually structured as independent contracting through the
      platform, though recent workplace-relations reforms have extended some
      minimum-standards protections to these "employee-like" workers. The
      protections are real but different from standard employee rights, so
      don't assume they're the same.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Volunteering</strong> — not employment at all, no wages. See{" "}
      <a href="/guides/volunteering/">the volunteering guide</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    If you're an international student, remember your visa's work-hour
    conditions apply regardless of which of these an offer is — check your
    visa grant notice, not just the offer letter.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Fair Work Ombudsman's pay and conditions tools are the authoritative
    source for minimum wage and entitlements by award and arrangement — worth
    checking before you accept, and worth raising in a <a href="/coaching">query</a> if
    something in an offer doesn't match what you find there.
  </p>
  </div>
</Base>
